# Pixel Canary — findings by Space Bunny Alpha (SELF-EXCLUDED: insufficient verified public data)

> **Why this file is `.excluded`:** as of 2026-09-27 I found **one** verified public
> benchmark family for `stealth/pixel-canary` (the Next.js Agent Evals leaderboard) plus
> a documented $0 price, but **no verified public data at all** for Reasoning, Tool use
> (as distinct from the coding eval, which is a proxy) or Multimodal. Three of the five
> quality dims would therefore have to be invented placeholders, which the template's
> SELF-EXCLUSION rule and `RULES.md` forbid — a fabricated number drags the mean for
> every reader. Notes only below; **no scores are asserted.**
>
> This is a *thin-evidence* exclusion, not a *no-evidence* one, and it is the most likely
> of today's folders to become scorable: the model shipped 2026-09-25, so independent
> evaluators have barely started.

- Source: Vercel AI Gateway (`stealth/pixel-canary`) — publisher unconfirmed
- Date: 2026-09-27 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

## Model card (facts only — verified, no scores)

- **Name:** Pixel Canary
- **Short description:** An **anonymous / stealth** coding model, listed on Vercel AI
  Gateway as `stealth/pixel-canary` and **free for a limited time while in stealth**.
  Vercel positions it as strong at application building and refactoring, and especially
  at frontend and mobile work — responsive layouts, app screens, navigation, interactive
  components. **The maker is unconfirmed** and Vercel makes no attribution claim.
- **Provider / access:** **Vercel AI Gateway**, model ID `stealth/pixel-canary`.
  OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions at `https://ai-gateway.vercel.sh/v1`; also selectable
  in Claude Code, Codex, `fx` and the Vercel model playground via
  `npx vercel ai-gateway setup`.
- **Release / knowledge:** released **2026-09-25** (Vercel changelog, 2026-09-25;
  commandcode.ai model record). Knowledge cutoff not published.
- **IDs:** `stealth/pixel-canary` (Vercel AI Gateway). The `stealth/` namespace prefix is
  Vercel's convention for an unattributed model.
- **Context window:** **262,144 tokens (262K)** — a single third-party provider record
  (commandcode.ai). Not confirmed by Vercel's own changelog; treat as provisional.
- **Modalities:** text out is confirmed; **no input modality is documented** anywhere I
  could find — commandcode.ai shows an empty modality list. **Adjustable reasoning
  effort** is documented (Vercel AI SDK playground). No image, audio or video input is
  claimed.
- **Pricing (as of 2026-09-27):** **$0.00 / 1M input, $0.00 / 1M output, $0.00 / 1M
  cache read** — free for the duration of the stealth preview (Vercel changelog;
  commandcode.ai confirms every request billed $0, credits never drawn down).
  **⚠ Privacy caveat, and a material one:** Vercel states explicitly that
  **zero-data-retention is NOT available for this model, and prompts and responses sent
  through it may be used for training and model improvement.** That is the opposite of
  the zero-retention posture several other free/stealth models in this dataset carry, and
  it should be weighed before routing any confidential code through it.
- **Architecture:** undisclosed — no weights, no parameter count, no vendor, no license.

### Raw benchmarks found

> Only the rows below have verified numbers. Everything else reads "no verified public
> score found" — that is the finding, not an oversight.

Agent / tool use:

- **Next.js Agent Evals** (the one real result; see the Coding section for the full
  breakdown): **90.3%** baseline and **96.8%** with bundled Next.js docs. This is an
  *agentic coding* eval, so it is only a **proxy** for general tool use — there is no
  Terminal-Bench, Tau3-Banking, GDPval-AA, Claw-Eval, Toolathon, MCP-Atlas or
  SWE Atlas row for this model.
- Terminal-Bench 2.1: **no verified public score found**
- Tau3-Banking / Tau2-Bench: **no verified public score found**
- GDPval-AA: **no verified public score found**
- Claw-Eval / ClawProBench: **no verified public score found**
- Toolathon / MCP-Atlas / SWE Atlas Codebase QnA: **no verified public score found**

Reasoning / knowledge:

- GPQA Diamond: **no verified public score found**
- HLE: **no verified public score found**
- LCR / MLCR: **no verified public score found**
- CritPt: **no verified public score found**
- Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index / BenchLM overall: **no verified public score
  found** — commandcode.ai, which sources its index from Artificial Analysis v4.3, lists
  Pixel Canary's Intelligence Index as **"not yet scored"**; BenchLM shows no model page.
- Omniscience Accuracy / Hallucination Rate: **no verified public score found**

Coding:

- **Next.js Agent Evals** (https://nextjs.org/evals, leaderboard last run 2026-09-25;
  Vercel changelog 2026-09-25; Eyestech analysis 2026-09-26):
  - **90.3% baseline success — 28 of 31 tasks passed.** Tied with **GPT-6 Astra (high)**;
    above **Kimi K3 at 84%**.
  - **96.8% — 30 of 31 tasks** when Next.js documentation is supplied through
    `AGENTS.md`; Vercel reports this as tying the leaderboard's top score in that setting.
  - **Scoring rule: pass@4** — a task counts as passed if *any* of up to four attempts
    succeeds. **Infrastructure failures are discarded and rerun.** The Pixel Canary row
    was produced with the **OpenCode** harness.
  - Task coverage: App Router migrations, data fetching, image and font optimization,
    caching, view transitions.
  - **Average duration 1,015.80 s** for Pixel Canary vs **251.89 s** for GPT-6 Astra
    (high) on the same eval set — roughly **4× slower per task** in that harness. This is
    a wall-clock observation, not a tokens-per-second measurement.
- **Independent field test, and it contradicts the leaderboard's read:** Eyestech
  (2026-09-26) ran Pixel Canary in **Cline Desktop v0.0.37** on macOS with the **Extra**
  thinking setting, one attempt per task. Result: **0 of 2 tasks completed.** A Three.js
  solar-system demo and a Python `report.py` CLI both produced reconnaissance reads and
  diagnostic shell commands, then a long thinking gap (**"Thought for 1201s"** and
  **"Thought for 800s"**) and the same terminal error — *"The run failed: The socket
  connection was closed unexpectedly."* No code, no build, no tests, no final answer;
  the source files were byte-for-byte unchanged.
  Eyestech is careful about its own limits and so am I: this is a **delivery/transport
  failure observation in one client**, not a measurement of coding quality, and it cannot
  overturn a pass@4 run in a different harness. But it is the honest counterweight to
  "ties GPT-6 Astra" — the pass@4 rule, the discarded-and-rerun infra failures, the
  4× duration, the client dependency, and the two aborted real sessions together say the
  90% figure is an upper bound on a good day with four tries, not a per-run expectation.
- SWE-bench Verified / SWE-Pro: **no verified public score found**
- LiveCodeBench: **no verified public score found**
- SciCode / AA-SciCode: **no verified public score found**
- Vibe Code Bench: **no verified public score found**
- DeepSWE / Coding Index / other: **no verified public score found** — there is no
  DeepSWE v1.1, no Artificial Analysis Coding Agent Index, and no Terminal-Bench row.

Long context:

- **No long-context retrieval reported.** The 262K figure is a single provider record with
  no MRCR, RULER or GraphWalks measurement at any window length.

### Negative-findings notes (what I checked, and what each source actually says)

- **Vercel changelog (2026-09-25)** — the launch note. Publishes the two Next.js numbers
  (90.3% / 96.8%), the pass@4 rule, the AGENTS.md condition, task coverage, the $0 price,
  the API/agent integration steps, and the no-ZDR training caveat. Publishes **no**
  context window, **no** parameter count, **no** vendor attribution, and **no** benchmark
  beyond Next.js evals.
- **nextjs.org/evals** — the actual public leaderboard. Independent of Vercel's marketing
  in origin; supplies the tie with GPT-6 Astra (high), Kimi K3 at 84%, the OpenCode
  harness, the rerun-on-infra-failure rule, and the 1,015.80 s vs 251.89 s durations.
- **Eyestech (2026-09-26)** — the strongest independent check available, and the only one
  that ran the model outside a curated leaderboard. Supplies the 0/2 Cline result, the
  two thinking-gap durations, the socket error, and the explicit methodological boundary
  that a transport failure is not a coding-quality score.
- **commandcode.ai** — a third-party model record. Supplies the **262K context window**
  (the only context figure found anywhere) and independently confirms $0.00/$0.00/$0.00.
  Its Intelligence Index and Coding index fields read **"not yet scored"**, and its own
  comparison table shows Pixel Canary with no score in any column.
- **Artificial Analysis / BenchLM** — no model entry, no rows, no Intelligence Index
  score. Nothing from the major independent text/coding evaluators has touched this model
  yet, which is exactly why three of the five quality dims are empty.
- **ai-sdk.dev playground** confirms only "anonymous large model with strong coding
  capabilities and adjustable reasoning effort… well suited to web and mobile app
  development" — qualitative, no numbers.
- Secondary coverage (margrop, startupfortune) restates the 96.8% Next.js figure and adds
  nothing new; the "tying GPT-6 Astra" framing in those posts refers to the 90.3% baseline
  tie on the leaderboard, not to 96.8% being a unique top score.

### Normalized scores (1–100)

- **Not scored.** Only **Coding** and **Cost efficiency** have verified inputs
  (Next.js Agent Evals 90.3%/96.8% pass@4 with a documented 4× duration penalty and a
  contradicting 0/2 field test; $0/$0/$0 pricing). **Reasoning, Tool use and Multimodal
  have no verified public data**, so asserting numbers for them would be fabrication. Per
  the template's SELF-EXCLUSION rule no scores are recorded here.
- What a future report should pick up, in priority order: (1) any Artificial Analysis
  Intelligence Index / Speech-to-Speech-adjacent row; (2) Terminal-Bench 2.1 or 4.0 and
  Tau3-Banking, which would convert Tool use from proxy to measurement; (3) any text
  reasoning row (GPQA/HLE) to fill Reasoning; (4) confirmation of the 262K context and
  whether image input exists, to fill Context window and Multimodal. If those land, a
  scored `.md` should be written and this file retired.

